      {
        "id": 218024,
        "name": "Escher",
        "username": "Escher",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/escher/{size}/4865_2.png",
        "created_at": "2018-05-09T02:32:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I gave a cursory look to see if somebody had asked these before but I didn't see anything right off, but yeah, I coulda looked harder.&nbsp; Well, if it gets asked a lot, it's probably a good indication of something that needs to be addressed in the full rules (if it wasn't already).</p><p>In terms of my sense as a GM, I'd definitely rule that a hinder always applies to the first roll it <em>can </em>apply to (narratively speaking), and if the creator and holder of a boost disagree about its use, the person holding it has the final say.&nbsp; As much as it'd be funny for Unity to Augment a weapon when the shooter didn't really want her to, it makes more sense from a 'speed at the table' standpoint to just hand it over and let the person the boost is on have control of it from that point.</p><p>The Starter set doesn't really talk about the narrative aspect of boosts and hinders (unless I missed it)... for example, if Bunker is all webbed up, he could theoretically still call tactical plays, providing boosts without being inhibited by webs.&nbsp; Or contrariwise, it doesn't make sense for a \"Long Range Targeting Scope\" to help out when Bunker is making tactical plans or punching through a wall.&nbsp; I would assume mods only apply to rolls that narratively make sense for it.</p>",
